* Dogfood task 2026-07-18: a hand-written 68000 assembly OS-9 program (a
* proper module, not BASIC09 or C) that opens a file named by the raw
* command-line parameter area, reads it via raw I$ syscalls, counts total
* lines and total characters (excluding the CR line terminator), prints a
* summary via I$Write, and exits cleanly -- with a graceful error message
* if the named file doesn't exist or no filename was given.
*
* Assembled with `r68`, linked with `l68`, run live on real os9exec
* (OS-9/68000) via tools/os9repl.sh, from the `claude` account's
* /dd/USR/CLAUDE (== host os9/h0/USR/CLAUDE, host-native, so authored on
* the host and converted with `flip -m` per using-os9exec-repl.md).
*
* Test input (filename "testinput.txt", the same 4-line file used by
* dogfood-c-line-counter.c, host-verified independently before ever
* running this program -- CR count via Python, not just eyeballed):
*   4 lines, 124 chars excl. terminators, 128 bytes total.
* A second, independently-authored file (testinput2.txt, 3 lines, 73
* chars excl. terminators, 76 bytes total) was also run through it as a
* cross-check, with matching output.
*
* Literal verified program output (via `/h0/CMDS/linecount testinput.txt`):
*   Lines:      4
*   Characters: 124
* Second file (`/h0/CMDS/linecount testinput2.txt`):
*   Lines:      3
*   Characters: 73
* Missing-file case (`/h0/CMDS/linecount nosuchfile.txt`):
*   cannot open file: nosuchfile.txt
* No-argument case (`/h0/CMDS/linecount`):
*   usage: linecount filename
*
* Real findings from live-testing this, none of which came from any
* skill file (68k/os9-68k-assembly.md explicitly flags exact psect/
* assembler syntax as an unverified gap -- see the dogfood report for
* how each of these was actually pinned down):
*   - psect syntax confirmed 6-operand, same shape as the 6809 RMA's
*     PSECT (name,typelang,attrrev,edition,stacksize,entry) -- taken from
*     a *different*, already-live-tested skill example
*     (basic09/basic09-vs-68k-differences.md's BASIC09-callable-subroutine
*     shim), not from os9-68k-assembly.md itself.
*   - `dc.b` string literals must use double quotes on this r68; a
*     multi-character *single*-quoted string ('hi') fails to assemble
*     with "*** error - value out of range ***" (single quotes are for a
*     single character constant only, e.g. #'0'). Not documented anywhere
*     in the skill.
*   - `OS9 <callname>` and a bare `dc.w <callname>` after `trap #0` both
*     assemble cleanly (r68 treats an undefined call name as an ordinary
*     external symbol needing no error at assembly time) but fail to
*     *link* ("Symbol 'I$Write' unresolved") because no `use`-able defs
*     file with the I$/F$ numeric call-code equates exists anywhere on
*     this disk image (searched all of /h0/DEFS). Worked around by
*     hand-defining the needed codes as `set` constants sourced from this
*     project's own os9funcs.h (Source/OS9exec_core/os9funcs.h) --
*     IOpen=$84, IRead=$89, IWrite=$8A, IClose=$8F, FExit=$06 -- which
*     matches the standard published OS-9/68000 call-code table.
*   - The command-line parameter area (A5 at program entry) is a
*     NUL-terminated string of the exact argument text, safe to pass
*     directly as I$Open's pathname pointer with no copying. This does
*     NOT match a naive reading of os9-68k-assembly.md's register table,
*     which pairs A5 with D5 as "param-area size" -- D5 did not behave as
*     a simple byte count in live testing (a raw dump using D5 as the
*     I$Write count read hundreds of bytes past the real string into the
*     process's environment-variable block). The reliable technique that
*     worked: bound reads to a small fixed count, sanitize non-printable
*     bytes to '.' before writing raw dumps to the terminal (a bare CR
*     mid-buffer otherwise resets the terminal cursor to column 0 and
*     visually erases what came before it -- this bit twice during
*     probing), then hex-dump the exact bytes. With one argument
*     ("hello"), the bytes were: 68 65 6C 6C 6F 00 0D 00 50 4F 52 54 ...
*     i.e. "hello"+NUL, then a CR/NUL pair, then "PORT=..." (environment).
*     With zero arguments, the very first byte is a bare CR (0x0D), NOT a
*     NUL -- so an empty-argument check must test for both to be safe.
*   - Raw I$Write does exactly what its own doc says ("No editing") and
*     nothing more: a bare CR (13) with no LF does not advance the
*     terminal to a new line under os9repl.sh's tmux pane -- it just
*     returns the cursor to column 0, so the next write overwrites what's
*     already on that row. Switching to CR+LF (13,10) fixed all the
*     visually-garbled/interleaved-with-the-shell-prompt output this
*     caused. (I$WritLn would apparently handle this automatically per
*     its own doc entry -- "LF append on output" -- untested here since
*     the task called for raw I$ calls.)
*   - `l68 -o=<name>` writes its output relative to the current *chx*
*     (execution directory), not `chd` (data directory) -- confirmed by
*     watching the linked binary land in /h0/CMDS (the chx at the time)
*     while `chd` was /dd/USR/CLAUDE. Not documented anywhere for `l68`
*     specifically (the skill's chx/chd split is documented for `cc`/gcc
*     conventions, not stated for the bare linker).
*
* See dogfood-report-asm-2026-07-18.md for the full account (assemble/run
* cycle count, exact error text hit, what was and wasn't in the skill).
*
* Module header fields (hand-defined; oskdefs.d on this disk has the
* Type/Language/Attr constants but not the I$/F$ call-code equates --
* those are hand-defined below from os9exec's own os9funcs.h, matching
* the standard published OS-9/68000 call-code table).
Prgrm set 1
Objct set 1
ReEnt set $80
Type_Lang set (Prgrm<<8)+Objct
Attr_Rev set (ReEnt<<8)+1
*
* Raw I$/F$ call codes (word form for the trap+dc.w dispatch pattern)
IOpen  set $84
IRead  set $89
IWrite set $8A
IClose set $8F
FExit  set $06
Read_  set 1
*
 psect linecount,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,0,800,start
*
* Entry: A5 = pointer to a NUL-terminated parameter string (the command
* tail exactly as typed, e.g. "testinput.txt"); confirmed live via a
* hex-dump probe on os9exec -- NOT CR-terminated as some 6809-derived
* docs might suggest, and NOT reliably sized by D5 (D5's exact role
* here did not match a simple byte count in live testing).
start
 movea.l a5,a4            a4 = saved filename pointer
 move.b  (a4),d0           check for "no argument" sentinel
 cmp.b   #13,d0             live-tested: a bare CR here means no command-tail text was typed (NOT a leading NUL as first assumed -- confirmed via a hex-dump probe with zero args)
 beq.s   nofilearg
 tst.b   d0                   defensive: also treat a leading NUL as "no argument"
 bne.s   haveargs
nofilearg
 lea     usagemsg(pc),a0
 move.l  #usagelen,d1
 move.w  #1,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IWrite
 moveq   #1,d1
 trap    #0
 dc.w    FExit
*
haveargs
 movea.l a4,a0
 move.b  #Read_,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IOpen
 bcs.s   noopen
 move.w  d0,d6             d6 = path number
*
* --- read loop: 128-byte chunks, count CR (lines) and non-CR (chars) ---
 sub.l   #128,a7           scratch read buffer
 movea.l a7,a3               a3 = buffer base (kept live through the loop)
 suba.l  a2,a2                 char count = 0
 moveq   #0,d7                  line count = 0
readloop
 movea.l a3,a0
 move.l  #128,d1
 move.w  d6,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IRead
 bcs.s   readdone            carry set -> error (normally E$EOF)
 move.l  d1,d3                 d3 = bytes actually transferred
 tst.l   d3
 beq.s   readdone              zero-byte non-error read: treat as done (defensive; avoids a hang if a driver ever does this instead of signalling EOF)
 movea.l a3,a1
scanloop
 move.b  (a1)+,d0
 cmp.b   #13,d0                CR = OS-9 line terminator
 bne.s   notcr
 addq.l  #1,d7
 bra.s   scannext
notcr
 addq.l  #1,a2
scannext
 subq.l  #1,d3
 bne.s   scanloop
 bra.s   readloop
readdone
 add.l   #128,a7             free the read-buffer scratch
*
 move.w  d6,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IClose
*
 lea     lmsg(pc),a0
 move.l  #lmsglen,d1
 move.w  #1,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IWrite
 move.l  d7,d1
 bsr     printnum
 lea     cmsg(pc),a0
 move.l  #cmsglen,d1
 move.w  #1,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IWrite
 move.l  a2,d1
 bsr     printnum
 moveq   #0,d1
 trap    #0
 dc.w    FExit
*
noopen
 lea     nofilemsg(pc),a0
 move.l  #nofilelen,d1
 move.w  #1,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IWrite
 movea.l a4,a0
 bsr     strlen               d1 = filename length
 movea.l a4,a0                 reset a0 (strlen advances it past the NUL)
 move.w  #1,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IWrite
 lea     crbyte(pc),a0
 moveq   #2,d1
 move.w  #1,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IWrite
 moveq   #1,d1
 trap    #0
 dc.w    FExit
*
* strlen: a0 = NUL-terminated string in.  Out: d1.l = length (excl. NUL).
* Clobbers a0 (left one past the NUL).
strlen
 moveq   #0,d1
sl_loop
 tst.b   (a0)+
 beq.s   sl_done
 addq.l  #1,d1
 bra.s   sl_loop
sl_done
 rts
*
* printnum: d1.l = unsigned value to print as decimal, followed by CR+LF,
* to stdout (path 1).  Self-contained 16-byte stack scratch buffer.
* Clobbers d0-d3,a0,a1.
printnum
 sub.l   #16,a7
 movea.l a7,a1
 adda.l  #16,a1              a1 = one past end of the 16-byte buffer
 move.l  d1,d2
 moveq   #0,d3                digit count
 tst.l   d2
 bne.s   pn_loop
 move.b  #'0',-(a1)
 moveq   #1,d3
 bra.s   pn_output
pn_loop
 tst.l   d2
 beq.s   pn_output
 divu.w  #10,d2                d2: hi=remainder, lo=quotient
 move.w  d2,d0                  d0.w = quotient
 swap    d2
 move.b  d2,d1                    d1.b = remainder digit (0-9)
 add.b   #'0',d1
 move.b  d1,-(a1)
 addq.w  #1,d3
 clr.l   d2
 move.w  d0,d2
 bra.s   pn_loop
pn_output
 movea.l a1,a0
 move.l  d3,d1
 move.w  #1,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IWrite
 add.l   #16,a7
 lea     crbyte(pc),a0
 moveq   #2,d1
 move.w  #1,d0
 trap    #0
 dc.w    IWrite
 rts
*
usagemsg dc.b "usage: linecount filename",13,10
usagelen equ *-usagemsg
nofilemsg dc.b "cannot open file: "
nofilelen equ *-nofilemsg
lmsg dc.b "Lines:      "
lmsglen equ *-lmsg
cmsg dc.b "Characters: "
cmsglen equ *-cmsg
crbyte dc.b 13,10
*
 ends
